# Dependency graph

In VS Code, put the cursor on a name and run Show Call Hierarchy.
Outgoing calls list what the value reads; incoming calls list who reads it.

## Values

[$4,800]:income
[$1,900]:rent
[$650]:groceries
[fixed_costs] := rent + groceries
[free_cash] := income - fixed_costs
[savings_goal] := free_cash * 0.4

<!-- On free_cash: outgoing shows income and fixed_costs; incoming shows
savings_goal and the tasks below that depend on it. Follow fixed_costs to see
rent and groceries one level deeper. -->

## Tables

[trips] := table
| city   | nights | rate |
| ------ | ------ | ---- |
| Lisbon | 3      | $140 |
| Porto  | 2      | $95  |

[hotel_total] := sum(trips, nights * rate)
[affordable] := hotel_total < free_cash

<!-- On the rate column header: incoming shows hotel_total, outgoing shows trips. -->

## Tasks :planning

- [ ] Confirm the budget :budget_ok @after(affordable)
- [ ] Book hotels :book @after(budget_ok) @estimate(45m)
  - [ ] Lisbon
  - [ ] Porto
- [ ] Set up the savings transfer @after(budget_ok, savings_goal > $500)

<!-- On budget_ok: incoming shows the two tasks blocked by it and the planning
checklist; outgoing shows affordable. On book: outgoing shows budget_ok and its
two subtasks. -->

Trip cost is [hotel_total]; planning is [completed(planning)]/[total(planning)] done.
